Complete OpenROAD PR 10662 from its own review: drop the footprint filter.

Applies on top of patches/openroad-10662-buffer-to-inverters.patch. This
one IS ours -- it is the study's "review completed" arm, and it exists to
answer a question the pull request thread already asked and answered but
nobody applied.

WHAT THE REVIEW SAID

The pull request's automated reviewer flagged this at high severity:

  "buffers and inverters ... will almost always have different footprints
   in Liberty libraries (e.g. buf vs inv). If -match_cell_footprint is
   enabled, this check will prevent the generator from finding any valid
   inverter replacement, effectively disabling the move."

A maintainer endorsed it in the same thread -- "This feedback looks
valid. It is better to remove the footprint checker for this move." -- and
the check is still in the code at head 0c49c2bd. The pull request then
went stale.

WHY IT MATTERS HERE, MEASURABLY

The filter is not hypothetical on the public designs. ORFS sets
MATCH_CELL_FOOTPRINT=1 for sky130hd and forwards it into repair_timing as
-match_cell_footprint from both repair_timing_helper and the post-GRT WNS
pass. In sky130hd's liberty every buffer carries cell_footprint
"sky130_fd_sc_hd__buf" (or "__clkbuf") and every inverter "__inv" (or
"__clkinv"). Both strings are non-empty and never equal, so the filter
discards every candidate inverter, best_inv stays null, and the move
cannot fire on any sky130hd design at all.

That makes the two arms a clean prediction rather than a hope: as
submitted, the move commits zero buffer_to_inverters moves on sky130hd;
with this patch, it can commit some. The asap7 and nangate45 platforms set
no MATCH_CELL_FOOTPRINT, so they are unaffected either way and act as the
control.

WHAT THIS PATCH DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO

The reviewer offered an alternative -- replace the footprint check with a
Vt-class match. That is a design decision for the pull request's author
and maintainers, not for a study, so this patch only removes what the
thread agreed was wrong and adds nothing in its place.

The one other live review finding, that BufferToInvertersCandidate does
not override estimate() and so the multi-threaded policy cannot rank the
move, is not addressed here. ORFS drives SETUP_MOVE_SEQUENCE through
repair_timing_helper into the legacy path, so estimate() does not bite in
the flow this study measures; fixing it would change the patch under test
without changing what is measured.

Retire when 10662 merges, is closed, or applies the change itself.

diff --git a/src/rsz/src/move/BufferToInvertersGenerator.cc b/src/rsz/src/move/BufferToInvertersGenerator.cc
--- a/src/rsz/src/move/BufferToInvertersGenerator.cc
+++ b/src/rsz/src/move/BufferToInvertersGenerator.cc
@@ -87,7 +87,6 @@
   // cannot fail partway through the netlist mutation. Filter dont_use and
   // non-link cells.
   const float target_res = resizer_.cellDriveResistance(drvr_cell);
-  const std::string& drvr_footprint = drvr_cell->footprint();
   sta::LibertyCell* best_inv = nullptr;
   float min_diff = std::numeric_limits<float>::max();
 
@@ -103,11 +102,6 @@
       if (resizer_.dontUse(cell) || !resizer_.isLinkCell(cell)) {
         continue;
       }
-      if (run_config_.match_cell_footprint && !drvr_footprint.empty()
-          && !cell->footprint().empty()
-          && drvr_footprint != cell->footprint()) {
-        continue;
-      }
       sta::LibertyPort* in_port = nullptr;
       sta::LibertyPort* out_port = nullptr;
       cell->bufferPorts(in_port, out_port);
